---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'Teleprompter Features | teleprompter.wtf';
const description =
  'Manual scrolling, local voice tracking, browser recording, Picture in Picture, mirror mode, local autosave, and practical presenter controls.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="content-page section-wrap">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Built for the take</p>
      <h1>Simple on the surface. Capable when needed.</h1>
      <p>
        Paste and present immediately. Local voice, recording, and compact-view tools stay behind
        clear controls until you need them.
      </p>
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/">Open the teleprompter</a>
    </header>
    <div class="feature-detail-grid">
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">01</p><h2>Smooth, steady scrolling</h2><p>
          The scroll engine uses elapsed time rather than frame counts, so speed stays consistent on
          standard and high-refresh displays. Pause and resume without jumps.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">02</p><h2>Smart Pace</h2><p>
          Microphone energy and cadence are processed locally to slow down in silence and match your
          speaking rhythm. No transcription and no model download.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">03</p><h2>Private Precision Beta</h2><p>
          An optional local model (about 67 MB on first download, kept in this browser) recognizes
          short temporary fragments and aligns them to your known script. The script, microphone
          audio, and fragments stay on your device.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">04</p><h2>A readable stage</h2><p>
          Control font size, line height, text width, alignment, and focus position. A near-black
          presenter keeps attention on warm, high-contrast type.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">05</p><h2>Prompter-rig ready</h2><p>
          Mirror text horizontally for beam-splitter glass, flip it vertically for an inverted
          setup, and use fullscreen where the browser supports it.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">06</p><h2>Local recording</h2><p>
          Record a display, window, tab, or camera when the browser exposes those capabilities.
          Microphone capture is optional. Preview and save without uploading.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">07</p><h2>Compact presenter</h2><p>
          Document Picture in Picture is preferred, followed by canvas-backed video Picture in
          Picture and an ordinary pop-out. The interface never promises unsupported behavior.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">08</p><h2>Fast from the keyboard</h2><p>
          Use Space to play or pause, arrows for speed, brackets for type size, F for fullscreen, M
          for mirror, and Home to restart.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">09</p><h2>Local, resilient editing</h2><p>
          Type or paste plain text with native undo and redo. Paragraphs are preserved, TXT files
          import locally, and autosave restores your script after a refresh.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <p class="feature-number">10</p><h2>Private by design</h2><p>
          There is no script or recording backend. Optional analytics are off until you choose them,
          and every user-script surface is masked from optional experience diagnostics.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Ready?</p><h2>Paste. Present. Done.</h2></div><a
        class="button button--primary"
        href="/">Start teleprompter</a
      >
    </aside>
  </article>
</Layout>
